Yes, read-only access is absolutely worth setting up — it's a sensible default and straightforward to configure in Postgres. A simple approach:

```sql
CREATE ROLE reporting_readonly;
GRANT CONNECT ON DATABASE your_db TO reporting_readonly;
GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA public TO reporting_readonly;
GRANT SELECT ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO reporting_readonly;

-- So future tables are covered too
ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES IN SCHEMA public
  GRANT SELECT TO reporting_readonly;

CREATE USER reporting_user WITH PASSWORD 'yourpassword';
GRANT reporting_readonly TO reporting_user;
```

That's genuinely the right call for any external query access.

One thing worth thinking through as you set this up: for reporting to be useful over time, having visibility into a broader slice of the schema tends to surface better insights than a narrow view. If your data spans multiple schemas or there are related tables outside `public`, including those in the read grant means the queries you get back won't have blind spots. Not strictly necessary to start, but worth a quick thought about scope before you finalize the role — easier to configure once than to keep adjusting permissions each time a new reporting question comes up.

Also, if there's any context you can share about what kinds of reports you're trying to build — finance, ops, user metrics — that would let us design queries that are actually reusable rather than one-offs. A little upfront framing goes a long way toward not starting from scratch each time you have a new question.

